# paprika — credentials
#
# Copy this file to ~/.paprika/.env and fill in the two values below.
# It does NOT belong in the repo directory; the plugin only ever reads
# ~/.paprika/.env.
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.paprika
#   cp .env.example ~/.paprika/.env
#   chmod 600 ~/.paprika/.env
#   # then edit ~/.paprika/.env and fill in the two values
#
# These are your paprikaapp.com account credentials — the same email and
# password you use to sign in to the Paprika app itself.

PAPRIKA_EMAIL=you@example.com
PAPRIKA_PASSWORD=

# Why the password is stored, and not just a token
# ------------------------------------------------
# The login token has no documented lifetime — nobody outside Paprika knows
# when it expires. The password persists so re-authentication can happen
# silently, rather than stopping mid-task to ask a non-developer to log in
# again. That is a deliberate trade-off, not an oversight: the file is chmod
# 600, and the README states the concession in plain language.
#
# The token itself does NOT live here. It is machine-written to
# ~/.paprika/state.toml, so refreshing a token can never clobber a password
# you just fixed by hand.
#
# Nothing else belongs in this file. Your preferences — allergies, dislikes,
# household — live in ~/.paprika/profile.toml, which is hand-editable and
# keeps its comments.
